    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The battle began by accident when an Allied fleet anchored in the Ottoman base to press an armistice on Ibrahim Pasha, and a clash at the harbour mouth turned into a general engagement.</li><li>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a> had 22 ships to the Ottoman and Egyptian 78, but their ships of the line, heavier guns and trained crews gave them superior firepower; most ships fought at anchor at very close range.</li><li>The victory did not end the war: it took a Russian invasion in 1828 and a French force in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnese">Peloponnese</a> before Ottoman forces left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>, and Codrington was recalled for exceeding his instructions.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Navarino has been called the last major naval battle in history fought entirely with sailing ships, though most of the ships fought at anchor. After it, some of the bronze from the sunken Ottoman ships was cast into a church bell in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carniola">Carniola</a>, now in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slovenia">Slovenia</a>.</p></aside>
    </section>

