    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">1821</span><span class="rf-text">Greek nationalists revolt against Ottoman rule</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">February 1825</span><span class="rf-text">Ibrahim Pasha lands in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnese">Peloponnese</a> with 16,000 men and overruns its western part</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">July 1827</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> sign the Treaty of London, demanding an armistice and Greek autonomy</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">September 1827</span><span class="rf-text">Codrington talks with Ibrahim Pasha off Navarino and extracts a ceasefire until the sultan sends further instructions</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">October 1827</span><span class="rf-text">The Allied fleets enter Navarino Bay and the battle destroys the Ottoman and Egyptian fleet</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">April 1828</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> declares war on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">October 1828</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egyptians">Egyptians</a> finally leave the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnese">Peloponnese</a>, and French troops clear the remaining Ottoman garrisons by the end of the year</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1830</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> Protocol accepts Greek independence</span></li></ol>
    </section>

    <section class="battle-field" id="how-it-was-fought" data-battle="battle-of-navarino" data-v="e24607c73c" aria-labelledby="battle-field-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="battle-field-title">How it was fought</h2>
      <p class="bf-lede">The field and the armies, phase by phase. Press Play, or step through with Next and Back.</p>
      <ol class="bf-phases"><li><b>The Allied squadrons enter the bay</b> The Ottoman and Egyptian fleet lay anchored in a horseshoe of three lines. The Allied ships came through the southern entrance in two lines, British and French to starboard and the Russians to port, slightly behind.</li><li><b>The Allies anchor in the crescent</b> The three British ships of the line anchored facing the Ottoman centre. The French took the side facing the Ottoman left wing, and the Russians the right end of the crescent. Allied captains had orders to fire only if attacked.</li><li><b>Fighting breaks out at the entrance</b> Dartmouth's boats were fired on when they tried to stop an Ottoman fireship, and the shooting spread along the line. Breslaw left the French formation to reinforce Albion and Azov in the centre.</li><li><b>The centre fights at anchor</b> Ships fired at very close range, with little room to manoeuvre. The French silenced the fort's battery. Asia concentrated her fire on Fahti Bahri, and Breslaw moved to the junction of the British and Russian lines.</li><li><b>The Ottoman front line is despatched</b> All three Ottoman ships of the line and most of the large frigates of the first line had been despatched. The small British and French vessels stopped the fireships, and only the first attack on Scipion got home.</li><li><b>The smaller vessels are destroyed</b> The Allied ships of the line, all still operational, fired on the second and third lines. Codrington tried twice to order a ceasefire, but his signals were hidden by smoke or ignored. Of 78 vessels, just eight stayed seaworthy.</li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="background">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Background</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-ali-of-egypt"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/muhammad-ali-of-egypt.jpg" alt="Muhammad Ali of Egypt" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-ali-of-egypt">Muhammad Ali of Egypt</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:ModernEgypt,_Muhammad_Ali_by_Auguste_Couder,_BAP_17996.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Auguste Couder, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> had conquered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a> in the 15th century. In 1821 Greek nationalists revolted, and by 1825 a stalemate had developed. In that year the sultan persuaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-ali-of-egypt">Muhammad Ali Pasha</a>, the wali of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> (technically his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a>, in practice autonomous) to send his Western-trained and equipped army and navy against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a>, and promised the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnese">Peloponnese</a> as a hereditary <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fief">fief</a> to Ali's eldest son, Ibrahim. In February 1825 Ibrahim led an expeditionary force of 16,000 into the Peloponnese and overran its western part, but failed to take the east, where the rebel government sat at Nafplion.</p><p>The Greeks appointed British officers: Major Sir Richard Church by land, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thomas-cochrane-10th-earl-of-dundonald">Lord Cochrane</a> at sea. In 1827 their regular troops numbered less than 5,000, against 25,000 Ottomans in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> and 15,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egyptians">Egyptians</a> in the Peloponnese, and the government was virtually bankrupt. The death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tsar">Tsar</a> Alexander in December 1825 and the succession of Nicholas I, a more decisive and nationalistic ruler, changed that.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> signed the Treaty of London on 6 July 1827. It demanded an armistice, offered mediation and asked the Ottomans to grant Greece a degree of autonomy under their suzerainty. A secret clause provided for consuls to be sent to Nafplion if the Ottomans refused, and authorised the naval commanders to take all measures circumstances might suggest, without taking sides. On 20 August Vice-Admiral Sir Edward Codrington, a veteran of Trafalgar and a sympathiser with the Greek cause, received his instructions.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="forces">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">The Two Sides</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>On Codrington's figures the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a> had 22 ships and 1,258 guns against 78 ships and 2,180 guns, excluding smaller boats and fireships. Codrington's flagship Asia led the British; the French were under Henri de Rigny, who kept his flag in the frigate Sirène, and the Russians under L. van Heiden. The Ottoman figures came through a French officer, Letellier. Another report by him gives two more frigates and 20 fewer corvettes and brigs, 60 warships in all, and James assesses the effective Ottoman strength as three ships of the line, 15 large frigates and 18 corvettes, 36 ships.</p><p>The Allies had 10 ships of the line to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a>' three, against which the Ottomans had seven double-deck frigates to one. Most of the Ottoman and Egyptian fleet were 58 corvettes and brigs with smaller guns, often ones the Allies had discarded. Most Allied crews were professionals with Napoleonic war experience; Ottoman crews had fought only the Greek navies, and some were found shackled at their posts. French officers who had trained the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egyptians">Egyptians</a> withdrew the day before, and Letellier was sick. The Ottoman fireships were probably the most dangerous weapon for the Allies.</p><p>Following a plan of Letellier's, the Ottoman and Egyptian fleet lay in a horseshoe of three lines, ships of the line and large frigates in front, with corvettes and fireships at the ends. The Allies meant to anchor inside the crescent, Codrington facing the centre, the French the Ottoman left and the Russians the right. In conventional doctrine this was an unacceptable risk, as the south-west wind could trap the Allies in the bay.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="course">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How It Went</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>At 1.30 p.m. on 20 October Codrington signalled the fleet to prepare for action, with orders to open fire only if attacked. At 2.00 p.m. the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a> entered through the southern entrance, Codrington leading in Asia. Ibrahim sent a message demanding that they withdraw; Codrington replied that he had come to give orders, not to take them. By 2.15 p.m. the three British ships of the line had anchored.</p><p>Fighting broke out at the entrance, and Codrington claimed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> started it. According to Allied sources, Captain Fellowes of Dartmouth saw a fireship being prepared and sent a boat, which was fired on. When a cutter sent to tow the fireship away was also fired on, he opened <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/musket">musket</a> fire, the French flagship Sirène did likewise, an Ottoman corvette attacked it, and the exchange spread into a general engagement. Most ships fought at anchor at very close range, mostly in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/attrition">attrition</a>.</p><p>Scipion was nearly destroyed by a fireship until Trident towed it clear. Sirène blew up the frigate Ihsania and, with Trident and Scipion, silenced Navarino fort. The French Breslaw moved to help Albion and Azov and helped destroy Tahir Pasha's flagship. Asia disabled Fahti Bahri and, with Azov, reduced the Egyptian Guerrière to a burning wreck; Moharram Bey had sent word that he would not attack, but a Greek interpreter sent to him was shot dead. Apart from the first, no fireship hit a target. By about 4 p.m. all three Ottoman ships of the line and most large frigates were gone, and Codrington twice tried to order a ceasefire, but smoke hid his signals or they were ignored.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="aftermath">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Aftermath</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Within two more hours virtually the entire Ottoman fleet was destroyed. Three quarters of the 78 vessels were sunk, many of them dismasted but repairable, and were blown up or burned by their own crews to keep them from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a>. Many men were trapped in burning or exploding vessels. Letellier gave Codrington Ottoman casualties of about 3,000 killed and 1,109 wounded, though Codrington thought the reverse more likely. Only eight vessels stayed seaworthy: one dismasted ship of the line, two frigates and five corvettes.</p><p>Codrington gave Allied casualties as 181 killed and 480 wounded, among them his youngest son, a midshipman on Asia, who recovered. The Russian ships Azov, Gangut and Iezekiil were disabled and the three British ships of the line were sent home for repair, but no Allied vessel was sunk. News spread across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> that night, with church bells rung and bonfires lit on the mountaintops.</p><p>The sultan still had around 40,000 troops in central and southern Greece. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> declared war in April 1828, and in August Muhammad Ali agreed to withdraw from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnese">Peloponnese</a>. Ibrahim at first refused but gave way after French troops landed in Navarino Bay at the end of August. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egyptians">Egyptians</a> left in October 1828, and the French cleared the remaining Ottoman garrisons by the end of the year. In September 1829 the Treaty of Adrianople accepted Greek autonomy as defined in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, but the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> refused anything short of independence, which the London Protocol of 1830 accepted.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="sources">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How We Know</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Much of the battle comes from Allied accounts, above all Codrington's report and despatches, and the claim that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> fired first rests on Allied sources. The Ottoman order of battle derives from Letellier, who gave the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a> two different totals, and the casualty figures are his, which Codrington doubted. The tables of ships draw on W. James's Naval History of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-great-britain">Great Britain</a> (1837) and the French Revue maritime et coloniale of 1883.</p><p>It is disputed whether Codrington meant to fight. His orders to his captains were to engage only if attacked, but his private letters, notably to his sister, suggest that he regarded a showdown as inevitable. His enemies accused him of plotting the destruction of the fleet from sympathy with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a>, which he denied. On an alternative reading he hoped the Ottomans would fire, to give him an excuse to destroy their fleet.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/russo-turkish-war-1828-1829"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/russo-turkish-war-1828-1829.jpg" alt="Russo-Turkish War (1828–1829)" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/russo-turkish-war-1828-1829">Russo-Turkish War (1828–1829)</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:January_Suchodolski_-_Akhaltsikhe_siege.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">January Suchodolski, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The battle has been called the last major naval battle fought entirely with sailing ships. It destroyed the Ottoman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> fleet and saved the fledgling Greek state from collapse, though it took the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russo-turkish-war-1828-1829">Russo-Turkish War</a> of 1828 to 1829 and the French expedition to secure Greek independence.</p><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> Codrington was a hero to the public, but in Whitehall officials thought he had grossly exceeded his instructions and weakened the Ottoman ability to resist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>. King George IV is reported to have called it "this untoward event". Wellington became prime minister in January 1828, and in June the Admiralty announced Codrington's recall, though he received the Grand Cross of the Bath and was refused his crews' prize money. He was given no further operational command in his remaining decade of service and spent his later years defending his actions.</p><p>In Pylos, Three Admirals' Square has a marble monument with profiles of Codrington, Van Heiden and Rigny, and British, French and Russian memorials stand on islands in the bay. The battle is commemorated each year on 20 October, with representatives of the three governments attending. Russian and French ships were named Navarin for it. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze">Bronze</a> from the sunken ships was used in 1834 for a church bell in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carniola">Carniola</a>, now in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slovenia">Slovenia</a>, which carries an inscription by the poet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france-preseren">France Prešeren</a>.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>At Navarino Bay on 20 October 1827 an Allied fleet of British, French and Russian ships under Codrington destroyed most of an Ottoman and Egyptian fleet at anchor, after a clash at the harbour mouth turned into a general battle. Greek independence followed only after Russian and French campaigns.</em></p>

